llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
108): a split that runs in host memory waits for each of its GPU inputs
separately. Not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
With MoE experts in system RAM, every layer of token generation hands the
hidden state, the router weights and the chosen expert ids from the GPU to
the CPU expert split. The scheduler copies each one with a synchronous
copy, so every tensor costs a full GPU round trip: on an RX 590 a 4 KB
device-to-host copy plus its wait takes about 25 us, and a HIP trace of
Qwen3.6 35B-A3B generation showed about 130 such copy-and-wait pairs per
token.

The copies into a host-memory split are now queued on the source backend's
stream, and the split waits once for all of them before it runs. Inputs
the queued path cannot take (non-contiguous tensors, a source backend
without asynchronous reads) keep the synchronous copy.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (RX 590, llama-bench, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B heretic
Q4_K_M, -ncmoe 99, tg128, two runs each): 23.9 / 23.7 -> 24.6 / 24.3
tok/s. llama-perplexity with 16-token batches (the generation path, no op
offload), 8 chunks of 512: PPL 13.3639 before and after, mean KLD 0, same
top token 99.95% (one near-tie in about 2000; run-to-run noise on this
build is the same size).

